1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,890
This tutorial walks you through the AEF SHS PAYGo model in twelve steps, in the order an analyst should work.

2
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:15,355
Every screen you will see is the real workbook, version 0.7, with its default inputs.

3
00:00:15,355 --> 00:00:20,223
Those inputs describe a fictional company in a fictional market.

4
00:00:20,223 --> 00:00:26,308
They are there to make the mechanics visible, not to describe any real business.

5
00:00:26,308 --> 00:00:29,122
Keep the user manual open beside you.

6
00:00:29,122 --> 00:00:31,632
It follows the same twelve steps.

7
00:00:33,132 --> 00:00:38,246
Open the workbook in Excel 2016 or later and let it recalculate fully.

8
00:00:38,246 --> 00:00:41,023
The cover carries a live status panel.

9
00:00:41,023 --> 00:00:42,776
First, the master check.

10
00:00:42,776 --> 00:00:46,137
It must read OK before you rely on any output.

11
00:00:46,137 --> 00:00:56,146
Below it, the readiness banner: three of twenty three gates are met at default inputs, and the model never calls a case investment grade.

12
00:00:56,146 --> 00:01:05,278
Then the active scenario, Base, and the credit data mode, Proxy, which means the credit figures come from the model's curves.

13
00:01:05,278 --> 00:01:12,365
Before you change anything, save a copy under a new name and keep the original as your reference.

14
00:01:13,865 --> 00:01:17,503
The Checks sheet runs twenty five integrity tests.

15
00:01:17,503 --> 00:01:27,180
The balance sheet balances, cash ties out, the DPD buckets reconcile to gross receivables, limits are respected and inputs are valid.

16
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:33,000
Each test returns zero when it passes, and together they drive the master check.

17
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,403
Below them sit five readiness flags, such as affordability assumptions not yet reviewed.

18
00:01:39,403 --> 00:01:43,041
They are kept outside the master check on purpose.

19
00:01:43,041 --> 00:01:48,716
They tell you how mature the analysis is, not whether the arithmetic is right.

20
00:01:50,216 --> 00:01:56,979
All hard coded assumptions live on a handful of sheets, and the colour code tells you where.

21
00:01:56,979 --> 00:02:01,611
Blue font is an input, the only kind of cell you should change.

22
00:02:01,611 --> 00:02:03,375
Black font is a formula.

23
00:02:03,375 --> 00:02:04,772
Never overwrite it.

24
00:02:04,772 --> 00:02:08,227
On the Inputs sheet, start with three settings.

25
00:02:08,227 --> 00:02:14,917
The first is the scenario selector, where one is Base, two is Downside and three is Severe.

26
00:02:14,917 --> 00:02:19,401
Then come the first model month and the local currency label.

27
00:02:20,901 --> 00:02:24,353
The Products sheet holds one column per tier.

28
00:02:24,353 --> 00:02:28,342
Tier one is a pico solar kit on a twelve month plan.

29
00:02:28,342 --> 00:02:34,401
Tier five is a three kilowatt peak inverter system on a forty eight month plan.

30
00:02:34,401 --> 00:02:39,694
Replace the names, sizes, loads and segments with your own catalogue.

31
00:02:39,694 --> 00:02:41,381
Keep all five columns.

32
00:02:41,381 --> 00:02:46,751
If you sell fewer tiers, set the sales mix of the unused tier to zero.

33
00:02:46,751 --> 00:02:53,884
The tier label refers to capacity only, so check it against the product's real specification.

34
00:02:55,384 --> 00:03:02,579
Consumer Risk sets each tier's instalment against the household income of its target segment.

35
00:03:02,579 --> 00:03:10,161
The incomes shown are illustrative placeholders and must be replaced with survey or customer data.

36
00:03:10,161 --> 00:03:22,618
With the defaults, three tiers sit above the ten per cent payment burden threshold: Tier 2 at 13.0 per cent, Tier 3 at 13.2 per cent and Tier 4 at 11.3 per cent.

37
00:03:22,618 --> 00:03:30,664
A high burden predicts higher default, so treat these flags as credit warnings, not only as social ones.

38
00:03:32,164 --> 00:03:53,672
The macro block sets the opening exchange rate, 130 local currency units per dollar, local inflation of six per cent, a tax rate of thirty per cent, an annual price increase of five per cent on new contracts, and a pass through of fifty per cent of currency depreciation into new contract prices.

39
00:03:53,672 --> 00:03:58,685
Do not set depreciation to zero because the currency has been stable.

40
00:03:58,685 --> 00:04:05,152
A PAYGo company buys hardware and borrows in dollars while it collects in local currency.

41
00:04:06,652 --> 00:04:09,629
The Scenarios sheet scales five levers.

42
00:04:09,629 --> 00:04:21,691
The Downside raises the default hazard by thirty per cent, trims collections and volume, raises hardware cost and sets depreciation at twelve per cent a year.

43
00:04:21,691 --> 00:04:29,173
The Severe case goes further, with a hazard 1.75 times Base and twenty five per cent depreciation.

44
00:04:29,173 --> 00:04:34,593
Real stress moves several variables at once, and so do these scenarios.

45
00:04:36,093 --> 00:04:40,384
Enter the total units sold in each of the five years.

46
00:04:40,384 --> 00:04:47,022
The defaults run from twelve thousand units in Year 1 to fifty thousand in Year 5.

47
00:04:47,022 --> 00:04:55,685
The model divides each year by twelve, applies the scenario volume multiplier and splits the sales by tier.

48
00:04:55,685 --> 00:05:01,837
Version 0.7 does not model seasonality, so read monthly results as averages.

49
00:05:03,337 --> 00:05:08,571
The sales mix sits on Products, and it must total one hundred per cent.

50
00:05:08,571 --> 00:05:10,045
A check enforces it.

51
00:05:10,045 --> 00:05:14,173
Be careful when you shift the mix towards Tiers 4 and 5.

52
00:05:14,173 --> 00:05:21,103
It raises value sharply, but those tiers usually have the least repayment history behind them.

53
00:05:22,603 --> 00:05:29,361
Each price plan has four inputs: cash price, deposit, daily rate and tenor.

54
00:05:29,361 --> 00:05:30,442
Take Tier 2.

55
00:05:30,442 --> 00:05:44,228
A cash price of 39,000, a deposit of 4,000 and a daily rate of 77 over 24 months give a monthly instalment of 2,342 and a total contract value of 60,210.

56
00:05:44,228 --> 00:05:53,329
The PAYGo premium over the cash price is 21,210, and the implied annual rate is about fifty per cent.

57
00:05:53,329 --> 00:06:00,808
That rate is the figure a regulator or a journalist will compute, so know it first.

58
00:06:00,808 --> 00:06:11,171
Hardware revenue is booked at the cash price at sale, and the premium is earned as financing income over the tenor.

59
00:06:12,671 --> 00:06:20,248
Results based financing has four designs: sales based, repayment linked, ownership linked and hybrid.

60
00:06:20,248 --> 00:06:29,850
Ownership linked RBF pays zero by design until validated ownership data have been loaded and this evidence switch is set to one.

61
00:06:29,850 --> 00:06:34,501
The model will not manufacture that outcome from proxy curves.

62
00:06:36,001 --> 00:06:40,340
Hardware is entered as the dollar FOB cost per unit.

63
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:50,436
The model adds freight and duty, converts at the exchange rate of the month and applies the scenario hardware cost lever.

64
00:06:50,436 --> 00:06:57,612
For Tier 2, a cost of 150 dollars becomes a landed cost of 23,400 at the opening rate.

65
00:06:57,612 --> 00:07:03,036
Installation, warranty and the acquisition cost are set per tier.

66
00:07:03,036 --> 00:07:08,126
Do not enter a local price already converted at today's rate.

67
00:07:08,126 --> 00:07:11,129
That would count depreciation twice.

68
00:07:12,629 --> 00:07:15,488
Operating costs follow their drivers.

69
00:07:15,488 --> 00:07:19,197
Payment fees are two per cent of cash collected.

70
00:07:19,197 --> 00:07:24,683
Customer service and collections cost 150 per active account per month.

71
00:07:24,683 --> 00:07:30,247
Staff and general costs are fixed monthly amounts, indexed to inflation.

72
00:07:30,247 --> 00:07:33,569
Size the central costs for the volume plan.

73
00:07:33,569 --> 00:07:41,528
A book of seventy thousand active accounts needs a collections team, a call centre and a data function.

74
00:07:43,028 --> 00:07:57,032
Four sources fund the plan: initial equity, a dollar term loan revalued every month, a local currency receivables facility, and an automatic equity top up whenever cash would fall below the minimum.

75
00:07:57,032 --> 00:08:05,025
The facility is drawn only to hold the minimum cash balance, up to the lower of its limit and the borrowing base.

76
00:08:05,025 --> 00:08:08,561
The cumulative top up is your funding requirement.

77
00:08:08,561 --> 00:08:11,673
It is equity you must raise, not free money.

78
00:08:13,173 --> 00:08:16,421
Credit Assumptions holds the definitions.

79
00:08:16,421 --> 00:08:18,719
Default at 180 days past due.

80
00:08:18,719 --> 00:08:26,165
Stage 2 from thirty days and Stage 3 from ninety days for the indicative expected credit loss.

81
00:08:26,165 --> 00:08:30,443
Borrowing base eligibility up to thirty days past due.

82
00:08:30,443 --> 00:08:34,800
A recovery cost of fifteen per cent of resale proceeds.

83
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:45,336
The binding constraint on the facility is usually the borrowing base, not the limit, and it shrinks when credit quality deteriorates.

84
00:08:46,836 --> 00:08:50,879
The Annual sheet sums the monthly statements by year.

85
00:08:50,879 --> 00:08:56,599
Check the revenue mix between hardware, financing income and other revenue.

86
00:08:56,599 --> 00:09:07,278
Expected credit losses are charged when each contract is originated, so a growing book carries the full lifetime loss of its newest cohorts.

87
00:09:07,278 --> 00:09:11,855
In the default Base case, EBITDA turns positive in month 21.

88
00:09:13,355 --> 00:09:16,067
Now the balance sheet and cash flow.

89
00:09:16,067 --> 00:09:19,760
The balance check must read zero in every period.

90
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:24,884
Operating cash flow stays negative while the receivables book grows.

91
00:09:24,884 --> 00:09:31,289
In the default case it stays positive only from month 51, thirty months after EBITDA.

92
00:09:31,289 --> 00:09:35,961
A PAYGo company can report a profit and still run out of cash.

93
00:09:35,961 --> 00:09:41,763
The cash flow statement and the funding requirement are the figures to trust.

94
00:09:43,263 --> 00:09:51,208
Switch the selector between Base, Downside and Severe and read the investment summary each time.

95
00:09:51,208 --> 00:09:56,339
The Sensitivity sheet records fifteen cases at default inputs.

96
00:09:56,339 --> 00:09:59,566
The Base investor IRR is 46.4 per cent.

97
00:09:59,566 --> 00:10:06,518
In the Downside the IRR is minus 22.9 per cent, and the Severe case is a total loss.

98
00:10:06,518 --> 00:10:08,670
Look at the currency rows.

99
00:10:08,670 --> 00:10:20,504
Twenty per cent depreciation a year takes the Base IRR to minus sixteen per cent, and freezing prices on new contracts cuts it to 7.9 per cent.

100
00:10:20,504 --> 00:10:25,056
Remember to reset the selector to Base before you save.

101
00:10:26,556 --> 00:10:44,865
Credit Portfolio consolidates the five tiers month by month: gross receivables by DPD bucket, the collection ratio, PD and LGD proxies, the indicative stage based ECL, and the borrowing base with its headroom against the facility.

102
00:10:44,865 --> 00:10:48,924
The buckets reconcile exactly to gross receivables.

103
00:10:48,924 --> 00:10:51,949
This is the view a lender reads first.

104
00:10:53,449 --> 00:10:58,169
Covenants tests each threshold every month and flags breaches.

105
00:10:58,169 --> 00:11:08,978
In the default Base case the lowest trailing three month collection rate is 72.9 per cent against a minimum of seventy, and no month breaches.

106
00:11:08,978 --> 00:11:13,241
Look at the headroom, not only at the pass or fail flag.

107
00:11:13,241 --> 00:11:19,407
The annual DSCR sits on the KPIs sheet, and it is a poor test for a growing book.

108
00:11:20,907 --> 00:11:23,295
Now load the company's own data.

109
00:11:23,295 --> 00:11:35,608
Credit Input takes one block per tier and up to sixty months: month end balances and DPD buckets, and monthly flows such as originations, collections and write offs.

110
00:11:35,608 --> 00:11:42,100
The screen shows the SolaraPay case workbook, whose history is synthetic teaching data.

111
00:11:42,100 --> 00:11:45,458
The buckets must add up to gross receivables.

112
00:11:45,458 --> 00:11:48,443
Then set the credit data mode to Actual.

113
00:11:48,443 --> 00:11:54,563
Reporting switches to the company's history, while projections stay on the curves.

114
00:11:56,063 --> 00:12:00,908
Vintage Dashboard sets the observed cohorts against the plan curves.

115
00:12:00,908 --> 00:12:06,395
In the SolaraPay case, every tier with history repays below plan at month 12.

116
00:12:06,395 --> 00:12:13,449
When the curves diverge, recalibrate the default hazard and collection rates on the Products sheet.

117
00:12:13,449 --> 00:12:21,572
Lenders lend against data, and twelve or more months of clean cohort history is the fastest route to better terms.

118
00:12:23,072 --> 00:12:25,446
Valuation holds three views.

119
00:12:25,446 --> 00:12:40,280
The DCF of free cash flow gives an enterprise value of about 15.6 million dollars, with a terminal value larger than the whole value, which is normal for a growing PAYGo book.

120
00:12:40,280 --> 00:12:49,436
The exit value at six times EBITDA gives an equity value of about 80.6 million dollars at the end of Year 5.

121
00:12:49,436 --> 00:12:58,506
The investor's stake of one third then returns an IRR of 46.4 per cent and 6.7 times the money, in dollars.

122
00:12:58,506 --> 00:13:04,271
Present the DCF and the exit value side by side and explain the gap.

123
00:13:05,771 --> 00:13:10,440
Unit Economics answers whether each sale creates value.

124
00:13:10,440 --> 00:13:24,107
For Tier 2 at default inputs, the expected loss is about 35.6 per cent of scheduled instalments, LTV to CAC is 4.8 times, and the cash payback is fifteen months.

125
00:13:24,107 --> 00:13:28,267
Read the ratio with the payback and the unit IRR.

126
00:13:28,267 --> 00:13:35,397
A strong ratio on a product with no repayment history is a hypothesis, not a result.

127
00:13:36,897 --> 00:13:40,896
Calibration turns graded benchmarks into questions.

128
00:13:40,896 --> 00:13:55,008
At default inputs, the Year 5 net margin is about six times the M-KOPA reference for financial year 2024, a reference marked provisional because published revenue figures conflict.

129
00:13:55,008 --> 00:13:59,869
The sheet asks you to justify the cost and credit assumptions.

130
00:13:59,869 --> 00:14:02,769
It never changes an input on its own.

131
00:14:04,269 --> 00:14:06,400
Finally, Investment Readiness.

132
00:14:06,400 --> 00:14:18,329
Twenty three gates, automatic where the model can test them and manual where they need outside evidence, such as legal review or the auditor's view of the ECL approach.

133
00:14:18,329 --> 00:14:25,075
The banner reads three of twenty three at default inputs, and it never claims investment grade.

134
00:14:25,075 --> 00:14:33,171
Every gate not yet met should become a condition precedent, a covenant or an accepted risk in the investment memo.

135
00:14:34,671 --> 00:14:48,049
The Investment Summary brings it together on one page for the active scenario: the operating trajectory, the funding requirement, valuation and returns, the lender view and unit economics by tier.

136
00:14:48,049 --> 00:14:57,196
Build the memo from this page and the sheets behind it, using Template T01, and check every figure you quote against its source sheet.

137
00:14:58,696 --> 00:15:01,031
That completes the twelve steps.

138
00:15:01,031 --> 00:15:02,855
Three habits matter most.

139
00:15:02,855 --> 00:15:06,868
Never use an output while the master check reads error.

140
00:15:06,868 --> 00:15:11,465
Load the company's own history before you trust any projection.

141
00:15:11,465 --> 00:15:14,895
And read the Downside as carefully as the Base.

142
00:15:14,895 --> 00:15:21,680
The user manual, the case study, the templates and the decision tools take each step further.

143
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:23,359
Thank you for watching.
